plain.li:
[po:verse,!condolences,season:summer] The days are long and the work is longer,
the sun is strong and the drink is stronger;
if ever you pass this way in the heat,
there is shade by the well and a cool place to eat.

[po:work,c:sea|home:coast,!condolences] Haul away, the tide is turning,
haul away, the nets are full;
the gulls are loud and the lamps are burning,
and the old boat's lighter with every pull.

Haul away for the cold grey water,
haul away for the bread and the salt;
if the sea takes more than it ought to,
it's the sea's own fault.

[po:song,w:tribal,!condolences] Quiet, quiet, the {ANIMAL} is near,
we are the grass, we are not here;
quiet, quiet, the bow is bent;
thank you, brother, for what you have lent.

[po:drinking,light,!p:sober,!condolences,c:highlife] Pour the good bottle, the one that we keep,
the night is too young to be wasted on sleep;
let the wise ones be wise and the sober ones stand,
we'll be wise in the morning, with heads in our hands.

[olde,po:nonsense,light,!condolences,theirs] There was a monk of {TOWN}
that kept a hen in his hood;
she laid an egg at every prayer,
which all agreed was good.

[po:verse,!condolences,season:autumn] The geese went over in the night;
I heard them, though I could not see;
they called and called the whole way south,
as if they meant the call for me.

[po:war,!condolences,c:martial] Stand in the line and lock the shield,
the one beside you is the field;
if that one falls, then you stand twice,
and that is all of the advice.

[po:song,!condolences,their:grovekeepers] For every tree that falls, a seed;
for every seed, a hand;
for every hand, a year of rain,
and then a tree will stand.
